  body: "# Purpose and ownership in deprivation of capital\n\n## Authored interpretation\n\nThe captured
    chapter links notional capital after deprivation to a benefit purpose, prior beneficial ownership
    and the claimant or partner; it requires the decision maker to establish a significant purpose for
    each act.\n\n## Scope and exceptions\n\nSpending or a gift alone does not establish deprivation for
    benefit purposes. The source distinguishes the JSA and IS wording, personal-injury trusts, diminishing
    notional capital, children and young people, capacity, enforceable debts and necessities. All facts
    and current legal applicability require review; no fraud or entitlement decision is made.\n\n## Inspected
    source passages\n\n- [DMG 29805, PDF page 158](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=158)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29806, PDF page 158](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=158)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29808, PDF page 159](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=159)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29809, PDF page 159](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=159)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29825, PDF page 162](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=162)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29826, PDF page 162](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=162)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29832, PDF page 163](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=163)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n- [DMG 29833, PDF page 163](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/69d8f3a496c86b751317022c/dmg-Ch-29.pdf#page=163)
    — DMG Vol 5 Ch 29: Capital.\n\n## Evidence and review status\n\nModel-assisted interpretation of selected
    passages in the 15 September 2026 frozen source snapshot. Specialist review is required. Source acquisition,
    source revision, legal effective dates and this record’s authoring time are separate.\n\nThis is an
    independent experimental publication, not an official DWP document, individual advice, an entitlement
    decision or an executable rule. Machine-extracted text may alter reading order, footnotes and characters.
    No PDF visual inspection, whole-chapter coverage or behavioural evaluation pass is claimed by this
    record.\n\n- `dmg-vol5-ch29`: PDF SHA-256 `a1b086fdad8d32d6273b35fb8895516b6957faacd05fd3b06e091032598a47b9`;
    extracted pages SHA-256 `b490507e3aaf6110ea0593fee2ee4d772fd5a1f9a75f4f91008734caec51cfd9`.\n"
  semantic_relations:
  - target: term/dmg-burden-of-proof
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The capital-deprivation passage places the burden of showing the benefit purpose on
      the DM, contrasting with the claimant’s disregard-evidence burden.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol5-ch29/0162
      quote: "29825 DMs have to show the claimant's or partner's purpose was to get benefit or more benefit
        if they\ndecide claimants or partners have deprived themselves of capital. Getting benefit or
        more benefit may\n\nnot be the claimant's or partner's predominant purpose but it must be a significant
        one 1. So when\nclaimants give away all their capital to a relative just before claiming benefit
        their\n\n\n1. main, or predominant, purpose may be to benefit the relative and\n\n\n2. intention,
        or significant purpose, may be to reduce their capital so they can get benefit or more benefit.\n\
        \n\n                                                                                         \
        \     1 R(SB) 40/85"
      locator: DMG 29825
  - target: term/jsais-capital-disregard-assessment
    predicate: http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#related
    rationale: The source’s deprivation exceptions include a personal-injury payment held on trust
      and diminishing notional capital; these qualifications must be reviewed alongside capital
      treatment rather than assuming every disposed asset counts.
    evidence:
    - page: page/dmg-vol5-ch29/0158
      quote: "29806 People are not treated as having capital of which they have deprived themselves if\n\
        \n\n1. the capital is a payment made because of a personal injury to them (including payments
        from the\nChildren’s Memorial Trust - see DMG 29418) and\n\n\n2. the payment is held on trust
        for their benefit1.\n\n\nThey are also not treated as having the amount by which notional capital
        is reduced under the\n\ndiminishing notional capital rule2."
      locator: DMG 29806
